namespace Employee.Infrastructure;
internal static class WorkerToolGuidance
{
    public const string MediaPlanning = """
        ART SOURCE: animations, edits and restyles take the project's own existing art as their source ({"$projectPng":"<path of the original sprite or atlas>"}), never an earlier generated output or a tool result posted on the task, unless that output was accepted as final art. When the original is an atlas or sheet holding several poses, do not animate the whole sheet: create a base pose of the one character with the sheet as styleGuide, then animate that pose. New art must match the existing art's scale, outline, palette and style; the manager's tester compares them side by side.
        PIXEL ART CONTRACT: create returns one still image. Use animation with a real source PNG and explicit frames/fps for animation; outputWidth/outputHeight are PER-FRAME sizes. Never request animation only in a create prompt. Plan separate jobs for different motions (idle, peck) and required GUI assets; one character pose does not complete all art. Missing or invalid outputs need corrected Manager tool steps, preserving usable work and existing budgets.
        ROLE OWNERSHIP: when a connected tool owns media generation, assign it to a tool step. Give a gameplay/code worker only its code deliverables; do not tell it to complete an entire document milestone containing art owned by another step. Assign the actual art import/UI animation integration to a downstream worker depending on the real tool output and needed code steps. Connected tools are called by the Manager, not by the worker CLI.
        ART DELIVERY PLANNING: For a request to create/change project art, generation and applying it to the actual project are one deliverable unless the user explicitly asks for generation-only, a draft, or approval before application. Do not invent a per-image approval/cancel gate. Plan native built-in imagegen plus import/reference updates in one eligible Codex WriteProject implementation task when within the existing write grant; avoid a separate review barrier after every generated PNG. For an explicitly selected connector, keep the authorized preparation -> tool -> WriteProject integration route and declare real artifact dependencies. Respect configured approval/continuation controls; never bypass them.
        Preparation and coordinator steps remain read-only/tool-free as assigned; they must not generate or import art. If project write access is absent, use the existing access proposal/permission flow rather than silently promote access or promise application. Bound the asset batch to existing time/call budgets; prefer applying each completed asset before starting the next generation. A paused/resumed implementation should recover valid outputs and finish their integration before requesting replacements. Final acceptance requires project-local assets, preserved existing identities/references where applicable, importer/layout settings and actual scene/prefab/style/code consumers, with actual paths and honest verification limits. Mere cache output is not completed project delivery. Explicit user acceptance requirements, real permission refusals, unavailable mandatory tools and user cancellation remain authoritative.
        """;
    public const string MediaDelivery = """
        ART DELIVERY CONTRACT (implementation): When the user requests artwork for the approved project, generating it authorizes the associated reversible local asset import and reference/layout updates within this task's existing WriteProject scope, unless the user requests generation-only, a draft or approval before applying. Continue from successful generation directly to integration; do not stop for an invented per-image confirmation or treat a PNG in cache as task completion.
        Process a bounded batch one asset at a time: inspect the reference/current consumer; generate via the real selected authorized art tool; inspect the returned file; copy it to a stable project-local asset path; configure importer/slicing/layout and wire the actual scene, prefab, resource, stylesheet or code consumer. Preserve existing GUIDs, save compatibility and unrelated art. For replacements keep a recoverable original or source-control diff. Do not leave runtime references to temporary/cache paths. Apply each completed asset before starting the next generation where feasible; if several outputs already exist, reuse and integrate them before regenerating.
        For usable images whose draft size/padding differs, adapt permitted importer/crop/slicing/layout settings rather than imposing an invented rejection gate. Do not waive explicit hard requirements or integrate corrupt, unrelated or unsafe assets. If a particular output is unusable, retain it with the exact reason, continue independent usable assets and use only already-authorized bounded correction attempts. Do not cancel the whole batch because an optional helper, optional build/preview or cosmetic preference failed; missing verification must be reported as unverified, never as a pass.
        Do not finish with only prompts, a cache path, a source-art folder or 'ready to apply' when local integration is authorized and possible. Report each real project asset path and its real consumer, plus checks actually performed and remaining exceptions. If time or cancellation interrupts, checkpoint produced files and completed imports so a resumed task finishes only missing work. Do not extend budgets, ignore user cancellation, bypass permissions, change security settings, install tools, call paid APIs or substitute a forbidden generation route. Only a real indispensable capability/access failure or an explicit user stop instruction blocks dependent work.
        """;
    public const string Recovery = """
        TOOL SELECTION AND RECOVERY: Choose actually available, permitted tools for the specific operation before writing a helper script. A filename extension is not an image format: a failed .ps1 helper does not mean PNG generation failed.
        Classify failures by the operation and its dependency: unavailable capability, unsupported command/script launcher, or an actual denied action. A PSSecurityException ('running scripts is disabled') establishes that the PowerShell script invocation was blocked; do not infer that imagegen, all file access, or independent authorized steps are blocked.
        Stop the denied action. Never change execution policy, ACLs, sandbox settings, approvals or managed controls; never re-run a denied script through another shell, encoded command or interpreter to evade a restriction. If the underlying operation is denied, do not reproduce it through another tool.
        Continue independent authorized steps using existing permitted capabilities, only within the current role, project, access and build/test limits. Examples, when available and allowed: native file-edit tools for text, approved file-copy tools for existing artifacts, engine Editor APIs for import metadata, and installed Python/.NET for permitted inspection or processing. Do not install tools, add dependencies, download runtimes or call paid APIs as a recovery shortcut. Tool-free providers and preparation/coordinator roles remain tool-free/read-only as assigned.
        Keep genuine image generation on the selected authorized image tool; do not replace it with code-drawn art or silently replace an explicitly selected connector. Preserve completed outputs and actual paths. Report exactly which step failed, which steps completed, and what remains unverified. Do not mark the whole task blocked merely because one optional helper failed. If an indispensable capability or underlying permission is unavailable, report that specific blocker without claiming completion. Honor an explicit user requirement to stop the entire task on any refusal.
        """;
}
